n July 14, after the withdrawal and replacement announcement, Brent crude futures briefly gave back some of their gains. Soon, they returned close to the high level before the announcement. At the time of reporting, Brent was up about 2.6%, above $85 a barrel. Its final settlement price was $84.73, up 1.7% from the day before.\n\n## In one line\n\nConcerns about US-Iran attacks, the sea blockade, and tanker safety continued on July 14, so the flashy 20% plan left the stage, but the chart only pulled back briefly before recovering: the policy turn and the oil chart’s “not over yet” movement happened on the same day.",
